{"paper_id": "paper-2378305c83249230", "doi": "10.1186/s13567-026-01785-z", "title": "Characterization of spontaneous gross and histologic findings in free-ranging Egyptian rousettes (Rousettus aegyptiacus) from Uganda with a review of reported pathogens.", "analysis": {"analysis": {"scope_and_question": "The review examines Marburg virus and Ravn virus within the species Orthomarburgvirus marburgense, focusing on Marburg virus disease in the Egyptian rousette (Rousettus aegyptiacus) as the natural reservoir host, to characterize baseline pathology and spontaneous disease processes in free-ranging populations from Uganda.", "evidence_base_and_review_method": "The review synthesizes evidence from a pathological investigation of 69 free-ranging Egyptian rousettes in Uganda, obtained during systematic efforts to identify filoviral reservoir hosts, using comprehensive post-mortem examination and histologic evaluation to document spontaneous disease processes.", "consensus_and_key_conclusions": "Gross pathological examination revealed lesions in 85.5% (59/69) of specimens, including scarring and fibrosis of wing membranes (85.5%; 59/69), focal fibrotic nodules (17.4%; 12/69), and lacerations (17.4%; 12/69). Histopathological analysis demonstrated universal microscopic alterations (100%; 69/69), with 794 discrete lesions across 28 organ systems, and the liver was the most frequently affected organ alongside multiorgan perivascular cellular aggregates (both 81.2%; 56/69).", "controversies_and_evidence_gaps": "Pathological investigations of free-ranging bat populations are constrained by insufficient sample sizes and selection bias toward compromised specimens, limiting the generalizability of findings to broader, ostensibly healthy populations.", "research_and_practice_implications": "Elucidating intrinsic factors modulating reservoir species health is essential to understanding zoonotic spillover dynamics and developing evidence-based strategies for mitigating future outbreak risks in human and susceptible animal populations."}, "summary_en": "This review focuses on Marburg virus and Ravn virus within the Egyptian rousette, the established natural reservoir for orthomarburgviruses, to characterize baseline pathology in free-ranging populations from Uganda. A pathological investigation of 69 specimens revealed high prevalence of gross lesions (85.5%) and universal microscopic alterations (100%), with the liver and multiorgan perivascular aggregates most frequently affected. Methodological constraints include small sample sizes and bias toward compromised specimens, limiting generalizability. The findings underscore the need to elucidate reservoir health factors to inform zoonotic spillover risk assessment and outbreak mitigation strategies.", "evidence_ids": {"scope_and_question": ["A2", "A3"], "evidence_base_and_review_method": ["A5", "A6"], "consensus_and_key_conclusions": ["A7", "A8", "A9"], "controversies_and_evidence_gaps": ["A4"], "research_and_practice_implications": ["A11"]}, "confidence": "moderate", "postprocess_audit": {"removed_duplicate_sentences": 0, "repaired_incomplete_fields": [], "evidence_replacements": [], "policy": "v10-exclusive-rhetorical-fields-1"}, "language_contract": {"policy_version": "v17.1-source-language-safe-english-1", "source_language": "en", "verified_english": true, "replacements": [], "original_evidence_preserved": false}, "status": "passed", "kind": "review", "provider": "mistral", "model": "mistral-small-latest", "attempts": [{"task": "paper_review_analysis", "provider": "gemini", "model": "", "at": "2026-08-30T23:30:50+00:00", "status": "skipped", "failure_category": "authentication_failed", "error": "authentication_failed"}, {"task": "paper_review_analysis", "provider": "bigmodel", "model": "glm-5.3-flash", "at": "2026-08-30T23:30:50+00:00", "status": "skipped", "failure_category": "model_cooldown", "error": "model is temporarily cooling down"}, {"task": "paper_review_analysis", "provider": "b